<!-- Part 14 e2e scenario: the ObjectType-sibling counterpart to
     test_vt_datatype_contradiction.NodeSet2 (DataType axis) and
     test_vt_contradiction.NodeSet2 (ValueRank axis): this one exercises a
     plain Object-component override to an unrelated ObjectType, with NO
     Virtual Type involved at all (see test_vt_override.NodeSet2's own
     comment: a plain Object override with no local extension needs no
     synthetic wrapper, the real declared type fully specifies itself).

     Declares two new ObjectTypes, MotorAType and MotorBType, as direct
     siblings under opcua:BaseObjectType (neither a subtype of the other),
     so _add_sibling_type_disjointness(BaseObjectType) asserts them pairwise
     disjoint. BaseType declares Mandatory "Drive" : MotorAType. SubType <:
     BaseType overrides "Drive" to MotorBType, a sibling, not a subtype:
     exactly the illegal move OPC UA's own component-override rule forbids
     (may only narrow to the same type or a genuine subtype; contrast with
     test_vt_override, the *legal* form of this same move).

     Because there is no local extension, no Virtual Type is minted; the
     restriction on SubType targets MotorBType directly:
       SubType rdfs:subClassOf BaseType,
           [ onProperty hasDrive; minQualifiedCardinality 1; onClass MotorBType ]
       BaseType rdfs:subClassOf
           [ onProperty hasDrive; minQualifiedCardinality 1; onClass MotorAType ]
     minQualifiedCardinality(1, C) is logically equivalent to someValuesFrom(C)
     under OWL 2 semantics (see _add_all_values_from's own comment), so with
     hasDrive now declared owl:FunctionalProperty (every has<BrowseName>
     property is, see _copy_class_and_property_layer: OPC UA's own
     BrowseName-uniqueness rule guarantees at most one filler per property
     per owner), the single permitted hasDrive filler on a SubType instance
     would have to be simultaneously in MotorAType and MotorBType:
     unsatisfiable once those two are disjoint. Neither fix alone catches
     this: without Functional, two different fillers could satisfy the two
     cardinality restrictions with no contradiction; without sibling
     disjointness, being in both classes is not itself contradictory.
     Confirmed with the real HermiT reasoner (check_consistency.py's
     expect-contradiction mode). -->
